1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:17,789
In the early hours of the fifteenth of January, 1978, a student named Nita Neary came home to her sorority house at Florida State University, in Tallahassee. She saw a man in a dark cap leaving by the front door.

2
00:00:18,889 --> 00:00:31,135
Upstairs, four of her housemates had been attacked as they slept. Two of them, Lisa Levy and Margaret Bowman, were dead. Within the hour, another student was attacked a few blocks away. She survived.

3
00:00:32,235 --> 00:00:43,627
The man responsible had already escaped from custody twice. Before his execution, he confessed to around thirty murders in seven states. The true number is unknown.

4
00:00:44,727 --> 00:00:55,564
His name was Ted Bundy. Many films and books have been fascinated by him. This one is about the women and girls whose lives he took, and the people who stopped him.

5
00:00:57,764 --> 00:01:08,260
He was born Theodore Robert Cowell on the twenty-fourth of November, 1946, in Burlington, Vermont, at a home for unmarried mothers.

6
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:24,464
He was raised at first by his grandparents, and reportedly grew up believing his mother was his older sister. In 1951 she moved with him to Tacoma, Washington, and married Johnnie Bundy, whose surname he took.

7
00:01:25,564 --> 00:01:36,913
He studied psychology at the University of Washington and graduated in 1972. He volunteered for the Republican party, and went on to law school.

8
00:01:38,013 --> 00:01:50,643
For a time he worked night shifts on a Seattle crisis telephone line. Beside him sat Ann Rule, a former police officer who would later write a book about him, The Stranger Beside Me.

9
00:01:51,743 --> 00:01:57,375
To the people around him, nothing in his public life suggested what he was about to do.

10
00:01:59,575 --> 00:02:09,196
In January 1974, a young woman in Seattle was attacked in her basement bedroom. She survived, with serious injuries.

11
00:02:10,296 --> 00:02:17,592
Then young women in the Pacific Northwest began to disappear. Most were students, and most vanished at night.

12
00:02:18,692 --> 00:02:33,327
Lynda Ann Healy, a university student who read the ski report on local radio, vanished from her room. Donna Manson, Susan Rancourt and Roberta Kathleen Parks disappeared from college campuses that spring.

13
00:02:34,427 --> 00:02:45,072
In June, Brenda Ball disappeared after leaving a tavern near Seattle, and Georgann Hawkins vanished while walking the short distance to her sorority house.

14
00:02:46,172 --> 00:02:56,327
On the fourteenth of July, a sunny Sunday, Janice Ott and Denise Naslund were taken from a crowded lakeside park east of Seattle, hours apart.

15
00:02:57,427 --> 00:03:08,029
Witnesses described a polite young man with his arm in a sling who called himself Ted, and asked women to help him with his sailboat. He drove a Volkswagen Beetle.

16
00:03:09,129 --> 00:03:23,103
Police published a sketch, and thousands of tips poured in. Several people, including his girlfriend and Ann Rule, gave the name Ted Bundy. But he was a clean-cut law student, and his name was lost among the rest.

17
00:03:25,303 --> 00:03:35,223
That autumn he moved to Salt Lake City to study law at the University of Utah. Soon, girls and young women began disappearing there too.

18
00:03:36,323 --> 00:03:51,213
Among them were Melissa Smith, the daughter of a local police chief, and Laura Aime, both seventeen. In January 1975, Caryn Campbell, a nurse, vanished from a ski resort hotel in Colorado.

19
00:03:52,313 --> 00:04:05,561
On the eighth of November, 1974, he approached eighteen-year-old Carol DaRonch at a shopping centre, pretending to be a police officer. In his car, she fought back, and escaped.

20
00:04:06,661 --> 00:04:14,299
That same evening, seventeen-year-old Debra Kent disappeared after leaving a school play in nearby Bountiful.

21
00:04:15,399 --> 00:04:30,332
In August 1975, a Utah Highway Patrol sergeant, Bob Hayward, saw a Volkswagen driving slowly through a quiet suburb before dawn. When he followed it, the driver sped off, then pulled over.

22
00:04:31,432 --> 00:04:40,648
In the car were a ski mask, handcuffs and a crowbar. Detectives linked the driver to the DaRonch case, and she picked him out of a line-up.

23
00:04:41,748 --> 00:04:53,844
In 1976, he was convicted of kidnapping Carol DaRonch and sentenced to up to fifteen years. Colorado then charged him with the murder of Caryn Campbell.

24
00:04:56,044 --> 00:05:08,567
He chose to act as his own lawyer, which let him use the courthouse library. In June 1977 he jumped from one of its windows in Aspen. He was caught six days later.

25
00:05:09,667 --> 00:05:22,317
On the thirtieth of December, 1977, he escaped again, through a hole in the ceiling of his cell. By the time guards noticed he was gone, he was on his way to Florida.

26
00:05:23,417 --> 00:05:30,628
He settled in Tallahassee under a false name. A week later came the attack on the Chi Omega house.

27
00:05:32,828 --> 00:05:42,001
Lisa Levy was twenty, and Margaret Bowman twenty-one. Karen Chandler, Kathy Kleiner and Cheryl Thomas survived serious injuries.

28
00:05:43,101 --> 00:05:52,061
On the ninth of February, he abducted twelve-year-old Kimberly Leach from her school in Lake City, Florida. She was his last known victim.

29
00:05:53,161 --> 00:06:05,727
Six days later, in the early hours, a Pensacola police officer, David Lee, checked the plates of a Volkswagen and found it was stolen. After a struggle, he arrested the driver.

30
00:06:06,827 --> 00:06:15,872
The driver gave a false name. Within days, police confirmed he was Ted Bundy, one of the FBI's ten most wanted fugitives.

31
00:06:16,972 --> 00:06:25,996
After a long search, Kimberly Leach's remains were found in April 1978, in a park near the Suwannee River.

32
00:06:28,196 --> 00:06:38,009
His trial for the Chi Omega murders was among the first to be televised across the United States. Once again, he acted as his own lawyer.

33
00:06:39,109 --> 00:06:53,531
Nita Neary identified him as the man she had seen leaving the house. A forensic dentist matched an injury on one victim to his teeth. Bite-mark evidence is now widely questioned, but the jury convicted him.

34
00:06:54,631 --> 00:07:04,487
He was sentenced to death. In 1980 he was also convicted of the murder of Kimberly Leach, and sentenced to death a third time.

35
00:07:05,587 --> 00:07:19,069
For nearly a decade he fought his sentences through appeals. In his final days, he confessed to detectives to around thirty murders, in what many saw as a last attempt to delay his execution.

36
00:07:20,169 --> 00:07:27,039
He was executed on the twenty-fourth of January, 1989. He was forty-two.

37
00:07:29,239 --> 00:07:42,380
Since then, he has been the subject of countless books, documentaries and films, often presenting him as a charming and brilliant monster. Survivors and families have said that image hurts them.

38
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:53,272
Kathy Kleiner, who survived the Chi Omega attack, has spoken publicly for years, asking that the victims be remembered rather than their killer.

39
00:07:54,372 --> 00:08:03,567
He moved between states, and in the 1970s police forces in different states had no easy way to share what they knew.

40
00:08:04,667 --> 00:08:16,891
Cases like his helped persuade the FBI to launch a national database for linking violent crimes across state lines, known as ViCAP, in 1985.

41
00:08:17,991 --> 00:08:37,788
Lynda Ann Healy. Donna Manson. Susan Rancourt. Roberta Kathleen Parks. Brenda Ball. Georgann Hawkins. Janice Ott. Denise Naslund. Melissa Smith. Laura Aime. Debra Kent. Caryn Campbell. Lisa Levy. Margaret Bowman. Kimberly Leach. And others, some whose names may never be known.

42
00:08:38,888 --> 00:08:55,571
Bundy was not caught by a genius. He was caught by a patrolman who noticed a car, a young woman who fought back and testified, a student who saw his face, and an officer on a night shift in Pensacola. That is the part of the story worth remembering.
